Hi,

Thanks for compiling this great package.

I'm currently trying to adapt the the bandit2arm model for my needs. Instead of
dealing with binary choices as outcome, my outcome is continuous; I'm trying
to set up a Rescorla-Wagner learning model together with a linear observation
model, i.e., I'm trying to assess how skin conductance data recorded during a
Pavlovian fear-conditioning task is modulated by punishment expectancy.
I'm switching to hierarchical Bayes because the MLE estimates I get from
nonlinear optimization appear to be too noisy to be usable for model-based
fMRI analysis.

Anyway, I thought the bandit2arm model should be quite easy to adapt for my
needs. The only free parameter I have is the learning rate, while there is no use
for the inverse temperature parameter anymore.

Thus, I replaced the categorical_logit distribution in the model section with a
normal distribution, reflecting the linear regression of punishment expectancy
on skin conductance data.

However, when I use simulated data, generated with a set of learning rates
for each subject, for some reason I am not able to recover the correct learning
rate from the hierarchical Bayes model. I was wondering if there is an obvious
mistake in my model section. I also attached the full model:

model {
  # Hyperparameters
  mu_p  ~ normal(0, 1);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 5);

  # individual parameters
  A_pr   ~ normal(0, 1);
  alpha ~ normal(mu_p[2], sigma[2]);
  beta ~ normal(mu_p[3], sigma[3]);

  # subject loop and trial loop
  for (i in 1:N) {
    vector[2] ev; # expected value
    real PE;      # prediction error

    ev = initV;

    for (t in 1:(Tsubj[i])) {
      scr[t, i] ~ normal(alpha + beta * ev, sigma_i);

      # prediction error
      PE = lambda[t, i] - ev[stim[t,i]];

      # value updating (learning)
      ev[stim[t, i]] = ev[stim[t, i]] + A[i] * PE;
    }
  }
}

Thank you so much.
Jane

